// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// window_controls.slint — minimise, maximise, close
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// The three buttons every windowing system has had for thirty years, drawn once here so the
// OS has one answer rather than three.
//
// It had three. labwc drew them on the standalone app windows from its own theme; WindowFrame
// drew a second, different set on the shell's floating windows; and a shell screen filling the
// display drew none at all -- so Settings, Files and Memory could be entered and then only
// left by a back arrow, with no way to set them aside, no way to un-maximise them, and nothing
// that looked like a window at all. Those are not three styles of the same control, they are
// one control implemented twice and forgotten once.
//
// # The shape
//
// 46 x 32, flush, no gaps -- Windows 11's geometry, and close enough to GNOME's that neither
// looks foreign. The wide button matters more than the glyph inside it: a titlebar button is
// the thing people throw the pointer at without aiming, and the previous 26px square with 2px
// gaps was a target you had to hit deliberately.
//
// # Close is red
//
// On hover, not at rest -- Windows, KDE and GNOME all agree, and it is the one place in this
// OS where a hover colour carries meaning rather than feedback. This is also the reason these
// exist in Slint at all rather than being left to the compositor: labwc 0.8.3 has exactly one
// hover background for all three buttons (`window.button.hover.bg.color`) and no per-button
// override, so under its decorations close CANNOT be made red. Checked against the strings in
// the binary, not assumed from the documentation.

import { Theme } from "theme.slint";
import { Icon, Icons } from "icon.slint";

component ControlButton inherits Rectangle {
    in property <string> glyph;
    in property <bool> danger: false;
    in property <string> tip;
    in property <length> bar-height: 32px;
    callback clicked();

    width: 46px;
    // The button is as tall as the bar it is in, so the hover fill runs edge to edge and the
    // top-right corner of the surface really is the close button. A 32px button floating in a
    // 48px header leaves 16px of dead strip above it that looks like part of the target and
    // is not.
    height: root.bar-height;
    // The hover fill reaches the full button, which is what makes a 46px target look like a
    // 46px target. Rest state is bare: chrome that is always lit competes with the app.
    // Theme.hover-fill is 3% white -- right for a list row you are scanning, invisible on a
    // 46px button you are aiming at. 12% is what Windows uses and what makes the target read
    // as a target.
    background: ta.has-hover
        ? (root.danger ? Theme.color-close-hover : Theme.text-primary.transparentize(0.88))
        : transparent;
    animate background { duration: 90ms; }

    ta := TouchArea {
        mouse-cursor: pointer;
        clicked => { root.clicked(); }
    }

    Icon {
        commands: root.glyph;
        // 20px of BOX, which draws a 10-12px glyph: these paths are laid out in a 20-unit
        // viewBox and none of them fills it -- Icons.close runs 5..15, so `size: 10px` was
        // drawing a five-pixel cross. At that size the software rasteriser turns a 1.4px
        // stroke into grey mush, which is exactly how it looked.
        size: 20px;
        thickness: 1.6px;
        x: (parent.width - self.width) / 2;
        y: (parent.height - self.height) / 2;
        // White on the red, because #e8eaef on #e05263 is the wrong way round.
        tint: ta.has-hover
            ? (root.danger ? Theme.text-on-accent : Theme.text-primary)
            : Theme.text-secondary;
    }
}

export component WindowControls inherits HorizontalLayout {
    // Whether this surface can be set aside at all. A shell screen can, because the taskbar
    // holds it; a modal cannot, and showing it a minimise button it will not honour is worse
    // than showing two buttons.
    in property <bool> show-minimize: true;
    in property <bool> maximized: false;
    // The height of the bar these sit in: 48 in an app header, 36 in a floating frame.
    in property <length> bar-height: 32px;

    callback minimize();
    callback toggle-maximize();
    callback close();

    spacing: 0px;
    alignment: end;

    if root.show-minimize : ControlButton {
        glyph: Icons.minus;
        bar-height: root.bar-height;
        tip: "Minimize";
        clicked => { root.minimize(); }
    }

    ControlButton {
        // Restore and maximise are different pictures because they are different actions, and
        // a button that does two things while looking like one is how people learn not to
        // trust a titlebar.
        glyph: root.maximized ? Icons.win-restore : Icons.win-maximize;
        bar-height: root.bar-height;
        tip: root.maximized ? "Restore" : "Maximize";
        clicked => { root.toggle-maximize(); }
    }

    ControlButton {
        glyph: Icons.close;
        bar-height: root.bar-height;
        danger: true;
        tip: "Close";
        clicked => { root.close(); }
    }
}
